WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.165
Lesson 2.8.

00:00:02.165 --> 00:00:04.360
The MEV supply chain.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:06.261
By the end of this lesson, you'll

00:00:06.261 --> 00:00:08.442
be able to understand who sees, orders

00:00:08.442 --> 00:00:10.902
and profits from your transactions, and how

00:00:10.902 --> 00:00:12.635
to get some of that value back.

00:00:13.935 --> 00:00:15.225
Here's the plan.

00:00:15.225 --> 00:00:16.910
First, the key ideas.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:19.625
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:19.625 --> 00:00:21.150
Then, your checklist.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.312
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:24.612 --> 00:00:28.043
The path of an Ethereum transaction today:

00:00:28.043 --> 00:00:30.755
First, you / your wallet send it,

00:00:30.755 --> 00:00:33.548
either to the public mempool or to

00:00:33.548 --> 00:00:37.677
a private RPC (e.g. an MEV-protection endpoint).

00:00:37.677 --> 00:00:40.908
Next, Searchers scan for opportunities (arbitrage,

00:00:40.908 --> 00:00:44.692
liquidations, backruns, sandwiches) and submit bundles.

00:00:44.692 --> 00:00:47.222
Then, Builders assemble the most profitable

00:00:47.222 --> 00:00:49.649
blocks from transactions and bundles.

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:53.697
Also, Relays pass blocks to proposers (validators), who

00:00:53.697 --> 00:00:59.419
pick the highest-paying block (proposer-builder separation, via MEV-Boost).

00:00:59.419 --> 00:01:02.270
On top of that, Order-flow auctions /

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:05.497
MEV rebates: some private RPCs and wallets

00:01:05.497 --> 00:01:08.948
let searchers bid to backrun your transaction

00:01:08.948 --> 00:01:11.424
and refund you part of the value.

00:01:12.724 --> 00:01:16.284
And Intent systems (Lesson 2.6) move competition

00:01:16.284 --> 00:01:19.709
to solvers, often with built-in protection.

00:01:19.709 --> 00:01:22.737
And finally, L2s usually have a single

00:01:22.737 --> 00:01:27.396
sequencer ordering transactions, which changes MEV dynamics

00:01:27.396 --> 00:01:31.744
(e.g. first-come-first-served or priority-fee ordering).

00:01:33.044 --> 00:01:36.749
Let's look at the diagram: The MEV supply chain.

00:01:36.749 --> 00:01:38.309
Take a moment with it.

00:01:38.309 --> 00:01:40.719
It's everything we've just said, in one picture.

00:01:42.019 --> 00:01:44.534
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:44.534 --> 00:01:48.825
A $100,000 swap sent publicly with 1% slippage

00:01:48.825 --> 00:01:52.685
could leak up to ~$1,000 to a sandwich.

00:01:52.685 --> 00:01:55.983
Sent through a protected RPC with rebates: no

00:01:55.983 --> 00:01:59.210
sandwich, and if the trade creates a backrun

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:03.513
opportunity (e.g. arbitrage between pools), you may receive

00:02:03.513 --> 00:02:05.175
a share of it back.

00:02:05.175 --> 00:02:07.832
Same trade, very different outcome, decided

00:02:07.832 --> 00:02:09.100
by where you send it.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:11.860
Here's your checklist.

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:13.395
Do it now, for real.

00:02:13.395 --> 00:02:17.432
First, Large trades go through a protected RPC, an

00:02:17.432 --> 00:02:21.770
intent system or an aggregator with MEV protection.

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:25.655
Next, I know whether my wallet's RPC offers rebates.

00:02:25.655 --> 00:02:29.703
And finally, I understand my L2's sequencer ordering rules.

00:02:31.003 --> 00:02:32.213
Question 1.

00:02:32.213 --> 00:02:33.438
What do builders do?

00:02:37.858 --> 00:02:40.845
The answer: Assemble blocks from transactions

00:02:40.845 --> 00:02:43.378
and searcher bundles to maximise value.

00:02:44.678 --> 00:02:45.923
Question 2.

00:02:45.923 --> 00:02:47.918
What is an MEV rebate?

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.500
The answer: A refund of part of the

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:57.983
value searchers extract from backrunning your transaction.

00:02:59.283 --> 00:03:00.553
Question 3.

00:03:00.553 --> 00:03:03.508
Who orders transactions on most L2s today?

00:03:07.928 --> 00:03:11.173
The answer: A sequencer, often a single operator.

00:03:12.473 --> 00:03:13.718
Let's recap.

00:03:13.718 --> 00:03:16.473
You / your wallet send it, either

00:03:16.473 --> 00:03:18.903
to the public mempool or to a

00:03:18.903 --> 00:03:22.930
private RPC (e.g. an MEV-protection endpoint).

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:26.559
Searchers scan for opportunities (arbitrage, liquidations,

00:03:26.559 --> 00:03:29.381
backruns, sandwiches) and submit bundles.

00:03:29.381 --> 00:03:31.491
Builders assemble the most profitable

00:03:31.491 --> 00:03:33.846
blocks from transactions and bundles.

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:36.811
Relays pass blocks to proposers (validators).

00:03:38.111 --> 00:03:40.626
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:40.626 --> 00:03:43.791
Next up, Lesson 3.0: Mastery Starter.
